WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.252 --> 00:00:01.910
- Everyone I knew, I felt like had

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:05.469
the most wonderful experience
after they had their baby

00:00:05.469 --> 00:00:09.302
but I didn't know how
to say I'm freaking out.

00:00:10.898 --> 00:00:13.970
- Postpartum depression to me is

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:17.220
the disconnect you have with your baby.

00:00:18.179 --> 00:00:20.503
- An overwhelming, uncontrollable

00:00:20.503 --> 00:00:21.934
sense of anxiety.

00:00:21.934 --> 00:00:24.161
- One of many mental illnesses

00:00:24.161 --> 00:00:26.161
that can affect mothers.

00:00:28.257 --> 00:00:31.621
- When I got pregnant, I
was instantly in a bad mood.

00:00:31.621 --> 00:00:33.881
In addition to that, I had fibroids,

00:00:33.881 --> 00:00:35.897
which are tumors that grow in your uterus

00:00:35.897 --> 00:00:37.278
and it was really scary.

00:00:37.278 --> 00:00:39.229
- I knew I had postpartum depression

00:00:39.229 --> 00:00:41.504
the first time when I went to go

00:00:41.504 --> 00:00:43.243
and pump my breast milk at work

00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:46.152
and I just broke down crying.

00:00:46.152 --> 00:00:48.281
- I actually remember
saying to my husband,

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:50.233
"You know, people kept
saying I'd be so emotional

00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:52.053
"and I feel fine," and the reality

00:00:52.053 --> 00:00:54.605
of the situation was that I was
completely emotionally numb.

00:00:54.605 --> 00:00:55.758
- Right after I had the baby, it was

00:00:55.758 --> 00:00:57.287
my husband's birthday
so I baked him a cake

00:00:57.287 --> 00:00:58.490
and I invited people over.

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:00.652
And people were like,
"What are you doing?"

00:01:00.652 --> 00:01:02.391
But I felt like it was my duty

00:01:02.391 --> 00:01:05.754
to celebrate his
birthday, to try to nurse,

00:01:05.754 --> 00:01:07.770
and then come back and
put on a happy face.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.176
- I believed that every parent around me

00:01:10.176 --> 00:01:13.086
knew what they were
doing and that I was this

00:01:13.086 --> 00:01:14.753
devastating failure.

00:01:15.768 --> 00:01:17.766
- When my son was four months old,

00:01:17.766 --> 00:01:19.863
and I remember breast feeding him

00:01:19.863 --> 00:01:21.650
but he wouldn't stop crying

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:23.635
and I got face to face with him

00:01:23.635 --> 00:01:26.135
and I screamed, "Stop crying."

00:01:26.999 --> 00:01:30.023
That was probably the moment I realized

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:31.843
I needed help.

00:01:31.843 --> 00:01:33.500
- It's a really defeating feeling,

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.208
knowing that this is something

00:01:35.208 --> 00:01:36.768
you have no control over.

00:01:36.768 --> 00:01:38.702
- I thought after that moment,

00:01:38.702 --> 00:01:40.295
"I just need to hold my baby.

00:01:40.295 --> 00:01:42.602
"I don't ever want this to happen again

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:44.391
"and something has to change."

00:01:44.391 --> 00:01:46.633
- Being a stay at home
mom was really isolating

00:01:46.633 --> 00:01:48.780
but I felt like I kind of came out of it

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:52.616
when I started exercising and
hanging out with other moms.

00:01:52.616 --> 00:01:55.086
- I started feeling better when I could

00:01:55.086 --> 00:01:56.842
wake up to my baby every morning

00:01:56.842 --> 00:01:59.784
and he just makes everything better

00:01:59.784 --> 00:02:01.328
and everything worthwhile.

00:02:01.328 --> 00:02:03.034
- To all the mothers going through this,

00:02:03.034 --> 00:02:05.586
surround yourself with people who get it

00:02:05.586 --> 00:02:06.821
because you're not alone.

00:02:06.821 --> 00:02:08.886
- I know there's so
much pressure right now

00:02:08.886 --> 00:02:11.015
to be like a perfect mom.

00:02:11.015 --> 00:02:14.102
You have to be honest,
you have to let people in

00:02:14.102 --> 00:02:15.777
and let them know what
you're going through.

00:02:15.777 --> 00:02:17.743
- If you are out there
and you are struggling,

00:02:17.743 --> 00:02:21.043
I want you to know that
it is going to get better.

00:02:21.043 --> 00:02:22.913
- I'm a mother and I'm vulnerable

00:02:22.913 --> 00:02:24.489
but I am capable.

00:02:24.489 --> 00:02:26.925
- And I am fighting every day.

00:02:26.925 --> 00:02:29.041
- And I'm doing the best that I can.

00:02:29.041 --> 00:02:31.235
- And struggling with
postpartum depression

00:02:31.235 --> 00:02:35.396
didn't make me weak but
becoming victorious over it,

00:02:35.396 --> 00:02:37.363
that's what made me strong.

00:02:37.363 --> 00:02:39.996
- We have lost way too many moms

00:02:39.996 --> 00:02:42.515
so please stay with us.

00:02:42.515 --> 00:02:46.823
Find yourself a community
and keep fighting.

00:02:46.823 --> 00:02:48.656
("There's Still Time")

